<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies</h1><p class="st-meta">1942 CE – 1945 CE · capital Jakarta</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Japanese military rule of the Indonesian archipelago from 1942 to 1945, which ended Dutch power and opened the way to independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Seeking oil and raw materials, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> from January 1942. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> forces surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalijati">Kalijati</a> on 8 March 1942. The colony was divided between the Japanese 16th Army on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, the 25th Army on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a> and the navy in the eastern islands.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Japanese interned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> civilians, banned the Dutch language and renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jakarta">Jakarta</a>. They co-opted nationalist leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a>, created mass organisations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/defenders-of-the-homeland">PETA</a> volunteer army, but also requisitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and conscripted hundreds of thousands of romusha labourers, causing famine and great loss of life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As defeat neared, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> allowed a committee to prepare independence, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> set out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancasila">Pancasila</a> principles in June 1945. Two days after Japan surrendered, Sukarno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Hatta</a> proclaimed the independence of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> on 17 August 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/japanese-occupation-of-the-dutch-east-indies.jpg" alt="Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: kahusi - (Talk) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Japan_(1870%E2%80%931999).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1942 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jakarta</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hitoshi Imamura</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Indonesian (Malay), Japanese, Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Japanese-occupied Dutch East Indies</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=japanese-occupation-of-the-dutch-east-indies" aria-label="Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7639 2408 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Japanese occupation of the Dutch East Indies lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7959 2664L8529 1145M7959 2664L7959 2663M7959 2664L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M7959 2663h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7959 2664h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jakarta. <a href="/atlas?year=1944&amp;focus=japanese-occupation-of-the-dutch-east-indies">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
